<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik-Shah I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1092">1092</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Malik Shah I, Malik Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Shah I was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> from 1072 until his death in 1092, when the empire reached its greatest extent. Assisted by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, he presided over a period of strong administration and cultural flourishing. His death was followed by succession struggles that fragmented the Seljuk realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i.jpg" alt="Malik-Shah I" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid Al-Din · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malik-Shah_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malik-Shah was born on 16 August 1055 and spent his youth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. In 1064 he joined his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>'s campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> with the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, and in 1066 he was named heir at a ceremony near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and given Isfahan as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. In 1072 Alp Arslan was fatally wounded on a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, and Malik-Shah was declared sultan.</p><p>His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qavurt">Qavurt</a> claimed the throne as the elder brother. Malik-Shah replied that a brother does not inherit when there is a son. In 1073 they fought the three-day Battle of Kerj Abu Dulaf near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, where Malik-Shah's army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a> and Kurdish and Arab contingents defeated Qavurt's Turkmens, although the Turks in his own army mutinied. Qavurt was captured and later strangled with a bowstring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> held near absolute power under the nominal sultan. Malik-Shah repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> in 1073, and campaigns in 1087 and 1089 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> up to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under Seljuk rule. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, George II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> submitted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1088 after campaigns in 1075 and 1078 and 1079. In 1086 the army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, Edessa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. In 1092 Seljuk armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>.</p><p>Revolts in the family marked the reign. His brother Tekish rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 1081 and again in 1086, when he was captured and blinded, and another brother, Toghan-Shah, rebelled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and was imprisoned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> became the chief residence, and he built mosques there and the Isfahan Observatory, whose work led to the Jalali Calendar. Omar Khayyam's poetry belongs to his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1092 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> was assassinated near Sihna by a man disguised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a>. One theory blames the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>; another suggests Malik-Shah had tired of his overmighty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. Malik-Shah then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and sought to depose the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadi.</p><p>He died on 19 November 1092 while hunting, probably poisoned, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> or by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> according to one view. He was buried in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. The empire then fell into chaos as rival successors and governors divided it and fought one another. The success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> is attributed at least in part to the political confusion that followed his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malik-Shah I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Western Kara-Khanids became vassals of the Seljuks after <b>Malik-Shah I</b> took Samarqand in 1089, and both branches later submitted to the Qara Khitai after the Battle of Qatwan in 1141.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Malik Shah I</b> (r. 1072–1092) the empire campaigned across Syria, Anatolia, the Caucasus and Central Asia and received the submission of the Karakhanids of Samarkand and Kashgar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Kilij Arslan I, held captive by Sultan <b>Malik-Shah</b>, was released after Malik-Shah’s death in 1092 and returned to Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by a Fatimid counter-offensive, Atsiz asked <b>Malik-Shah</b> for help.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-atsiz">Emirate of Atsiz</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1085 <b>Malik-Shah</b> sent its former vizier Fakhr al-Dawla ibn Jahir to conquer it, and Diyar Bakr passed to Seljuk governors and later to the Inalids and Artuqids.</blockquote><a href="/state/marwanids">Marwanids</a></li><li><blockquote>He later rebelled against his nephew <b>Malik-Shah</b> and was executed in 1074, but his sons kept Kerman.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-kerman">Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1082" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Malik-Shah I"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4403 4751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4403 4751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1082">What was here in 1082? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alp-arslan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alp Arslan</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 1029–1072</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/berkyaruq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berkyaruq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Berkyaruq</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1081–1105</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-i-tapar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Muhammad I Tapar</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1082–1118</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-sanjar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ahmad Sanjar</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 1086–1157</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I implored the Lord of Hosts that he would take from me my life and crown, if my brother be more worthy than myself to reign over the Moslems.&#8221;</p><footer>At the tomb of the Imam Riza at Tus, on the eve of battle with his brother (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 57) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it from the Persian historians, answering his vizier &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/735/pg735-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
